course: dictionary
lesson: 2661
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The same word can mean different things in different places. Context, the words around it, tells you which meaning the writer means. «For hundreds of years people avoided saying the names of God, the Devil and hell out loud, because it felt rude or unlucky.» Here the Devil is the main evil spirit, the enemy of God.
explain: Now read «Poor devil, he missed the bus again.» There is no evil here. A poor devil is a person you feel sorry for, so the word is used in a friendly, everyday way. Ask yourself: is this a story about evil, or a feeling about a person?
explain: Devils can also be animals. «A real Tasmanian devil does not spin, though it screeches and eats bones and fur.» A Tasmanian devil is a small, fierce black animal from Australia. The clues screech and bones tell you which devil the writer means.
fact: A devil can be an evil spirit in a story, or the Devil, the enemy of God.
fact: «A little devil» can be a fond way to call a naughty child.
fact: «Lucky devil» means a lucky person, and «poor devil» means someone you feel sorry for.
fact: The Tasmanian devil is a small, fierce black animal from Australia.
fact: «Speak of the devil» is said when someone you were just talking about arrives.
exercise: «He's such a little devil, always hiding my shoes!» What does little devil mean here? | *A naughty child, said fondly | A person who is ill | An animal from Australia | A cruel monster in a dark cave
exercise: Mum says, «Poor devil, he has a cold.» What does poor devil mean? | A wicked person who likes to cause trouble | *A person you feel sorry for | A lucky person
exercise: Which one is a Tasmanian devil? | *A small, fierce black animal from Australia | A wicked figure with horns and a tail | A tall tree from the forest | A type of soft cheese
test: «The thorny devil has tiny grooves between its spines that pull water from damp sand up to its mouth.» What kind of devil is this? | A naughty child | The Devil, the enemy of God | *An animal | A devil from a story
test: «Speak of the devil!» is said when someone you were talking about arrives. What does it show? | A demon has entered the room | Someone has been very bad | A devil from a story is coming | *The person you were talking about has just arrived
exam: What is the best way to work out which meaning of devil a writer means? | Guess the first meaning you see and never check | Change the word to a different one | *Read the words around it for clues, then check that your meaning fits | Skip it and never find out
